{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>From the field you shall eat</b> — by means of the field you shall eat from the house, for if that which grows in the field is consumed, you must eliminate from the house, just as is said regarding the Sabbatical year. This is the language of Rashi. And if so, the verse is not connected properly, for it should rather say: \"You shall not sow and you shall not reap, for it is a Jubilee, it shall be holy to you; from the field you shall eat its produce\" — not from the house. \n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said: \"From the field you shall eat\" — from that which the field produces of itself you shall eat, all of you, as written regarding the Sabbatical year.\n\nBut in my opinion, the verse says: \"You shall not reap and you shall not harvest in this year, for it is a Jubilee and it shall be holy to you. From the field you shall bring the produce and eat it\" — that you go out to the field to glean and eat together with the poor and the needy, both human and beast; it shall not be for you a time of reaping and harvesting, and you shall not gather into your houses and storehouses like the produce of other years.\n\nAnd that which they taught in Torat Kohanim: \"From here they said: one who presses three grape-cakes in one barrel — Rabbi Eliezer says: they may eat on the first; Rabbi Joshua says: even on the last; Rabban Gamliel says: whatever is consumed from it in the field must be eliminated from it in the house, and the halakhah follows his words; Rabbi Shimon says: all vegetables are treated as one for elimination\" — all of this is a midrash, because Scripture did not say \"from the field you shall bring and eat,\" but attached the eating to the field, implying that they may eat only in it. And it is possible that this is an asmakhta from their words, and therefore Rabbi Joshua was lenient regarding it and Rabban Gamliel was lenient in his reasoning. And I have already written this above. And according to Rabban Gamliel it is possible that the commandment of elimination is from the Torah, but the reason is that since it was initially absorbed from the outset, it is considered already eliminated."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The passage quotes Torat Kohanim (Sifra) discussing the law of bi'ur (removal/elimination of stored produce) during the Jubilee year; 'כובש שלושה כבשים בחבית אחת' refers to one who presses three batches of grapes in a single barrel, a case used to debate the scope of the bi'ur obligation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:59:58.131046375Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}